[Music]  on a cold night about three years ago in  a small office space I sat around  huddled around the computer with my  startup colleagues in deep anticipation  we had just launched the downloadable  version of an operating system that we'd  started it enabled you to put your  Android mobile apps on a desktop and  experience it we wondered if people  would use this product in different  countries around the world within a week  we had over 1 million people in over 180  countries use this product eventually it  was integrated into Chrome OS which is  used by hundreds of millions of people  how group of entrepreneurs was not here  in Silicon Valley nor in the United  States we were in the developing country  we were in Beijing China and that really  inspired me when I looked back on that  night because I myself from a developing  country you see 10 years ago I moved  from my home country of Zimbabwe to  China initially is planning to spend one  year learning how to read write and  speak Mandarin when I landed in China  and saw the remarkable innovation that  was there I decided that that was the  place that had to be and I said to  myself Towanda you need to stay here in  China to make that happen and be part of  it you see the image I had in my mind of  innovation was unit Packard in the Palo  Alto garage a few miles from here making  technology that would start in Silicon  Valley and trickle down to the rest of  the world and the world is changing this  used to be the place where this used to  be the only place we could get  opportunities to start an innovative  tech company what I realize is that  genius is equally this  tributed but opportunity is not there  was genius in Hungarian boy who survived  the Holocaust and a communist  dictatorship to come to the United  States and become the founding CEO of  Intel and the greatest manager in a  generation Andy Grove there was genius  in a Russian Jewish boy whose family was  persecuted in the 1970s and came here to  seek refuge and eventually co-founded  Google Sergey Brin there was genius in a  South African dreamer who came here  dropped out of his Stanford PhD in two  days and eventually co-founded PayPal  becoming today one of the boldest and  most iconic entrepreneurs Elon Musk  even in Steve Jobs whose father moved  here from Syria countless Chinese and  Indian entrepreneurs and engineers have  been the bedrock of talent in Silicon  Valley for the last few decades because  this was the only place where you could  find opportunity to build a great tech  company genius is equally distributed  but opportunity is not yet again the  world is changing more and more Springs  of innovation are rising up and black  places of the world that used to be  deserts of poverty and backwardness  we're able to see that over the next few  years the next billion people coming  online for the first time are not going  to be in Western Europe or in North  America they'll be in emerging markets  throughout Asia Africa and Latin America  will see a huge middle-class emerging  from raw as people migrate from rural to  urban areas to this mega cities in  Jakarta Indonesia and Delhi India and  thar Paulo Brazil as well as Lagos  Nigeria in all these places many  talented people will be able to stay at  home and build the entrepreneurial  dreams that they could only build here  we look as well at how ideas and crop up  in the most unexpected of places  growing up in Zimbabwe it is very clear  that we would save on gas by sharing  rides with each other we would invite  strangers into our cars so that they  would be able to hop in the ride and  give us a small amount of money and  together we'd bring down the overall  cost of the ride this is commonplace in  my country over a decade ago an American  entrepreneur named Logan green visited  Zimbabwe saw that this is the way that  we got around and was inspired to come  back to the United States and launch  Zimride a startup that provided a  carpooling service for students who at  the end of the school year could share  rides to go home this team and idea  evolved into lift the ride-sharing  application that many of us use and this  this industry of more than hundreds of  billions of dollars best taken over the  world  just by show of hands how many of us  have used ride has used lyft or uber  over the last month honey how many of us  knew that the idea was germinated in a  small southern African country not as  many as I thought Yeah right  as we can see ideas crop up in the most  unexpected of places a couple of years  ago I had the fortune of working with  many entrepreneurs who realized the  unique opportunities that come in  emerging markets I ran a startup  accelerator called Beijing tech hive  that's invested in early-stage  entrepreneurs in the mobile internet  these entrepreneurs were able to see  that in this world that we live in a new  paradigm of making products for and with  users needs to come into place it was in  May it was by a Chinese entrepreneur  that one of the most popular u.s.  apps for teenagers today tik-tok was  started it required someone who realized  what it really means to be mobile first  to have never had another product but  you have never had a desktop or laptop  as the only channel to the Internet  our world is changing much my  faster than we realize in parts of the  world that we used to say lacked  opportunity they'll continue to be ways  that entrepreneurs regardless of where  they are can make a new future and  create new innovation for us  I don't dare say that Silicon Valley is  no longer the preeminent center of  innovation in the world it is otherwise  I wouldn't be here and there's no  question that the United States has  created one of the greatest engines of  starting nurturing a businesses that the  world has ever known but we're able to  see that different parts of the world  are launching other opportunities and  hotbeds of innovation that continue to  be there and we have a choice to make we  can continue to over invest in highly  expensive startups that can't even  afford the garage as those  hewlett-packard entrepreneurs were able  to do or we could direct our capital to  parts of the world that desperately need  just that to take advantage of their  genius and their innovate in their  innovation we could continue to believe  that all great ideas will come from this  rich ecosystem a Stanford Silicon Valley  in the United States all we could  realize that if we open our minds and  our intellectually humble that more and  more ideas can spring out of these  places it is after all at a time now  today when we celebrate Apple pay and  Android pay as the latest innovation and  payments that we realized that it was in  over a decade and a half ago that in  East Africa where we lacked traditional  banking branches to provide to our rural  communities we had no option but to  leapfrog to mobile banking which is now  commonplace we have an opportunity to  open our minds to opportunities and  ideas that come from places  there are traditionally despised and  consider this backward if you are a  dreamer or an innovator regardless of  where you are you can in this world  that's highly connected start something  right from scratch the era of  trickle-down technology is over thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]

